BEGIN_PGML


Computing Taylor polynomials using the explicit formula is quite tedious. Another option is to use single variable Taylor's theorem. This may seem counterintuitive at first but it can be much more efficient. You only need a bit of care to properly justify your work.

To compute the 2nd Taylor polynomial of [` f(x,y) = e^{x+y} `] at [` (0,0) `], this argument has the main ideas.

---

1. _Since [` e^t `] is analytic everywhere and hence [` C^3 `] everywhere, single variable Taylor's theorem implies that there exists a real-valued function [` R `] defined on an open interval of [` 0 `] such that_

>>[` e^t = 1 + t + \dfrac{t^2}{2}  + R(t), \qquad  \lim_{t \to 0} \dfrac{R(t)}{t^2} = 0. \qquad (1)`]<<

2. _Then [` f(x,y) = P(x,y) + R(x+y) `] where [` P(x,y) = 1 + x + y + \dfrac{x^2}{2} + xy + \dfrac{y^2}{2} `]._

3. _By [`(1)`],_

>>[` \lim_{(x,y) \to (0,0)} \dfrac{R(x+y)}{||(x,y)||^2} =  \lim_{(x,y) \to (0,0)} \dfrac{R(x+y)}{(x+y)^2} \cdot \dfrac{(x+y)^2}{||(x,y)||^2} =0. `]<<

4. _Therefore, multivariable Taylor's theorem implies that [` P `] is the 2nd Taylor polynomial of [` f `] at [` (0,0) `]._

---

However, one line is missing some key justifications. Identify this line and what needs to be justified. Then fill in the details to justify this line.

[_]{$mc}
END_PGML

